ts and the signals tests against the clone. Both pass. The full `npm test` run did not finish in my window: it was still on `test/gloss.test.ts` when I stopped, so I have no full-suite result. I did not get a typecheck result either, because that output was lost in a backgrounded command.\n\n### Findings, most important first\n\n1. **Mismatch, read.** The gloss skill, which the new warning tells the user to read, contradicts the warning. `skills/gloss/SKILL.md:112` says \"Both fields are optional. Without them `show` derives a value from the diff\", and the README still says \"Leaving `impact` or `attention` out is always allowed\". The warning says to \"rate impact on every group except a pure rename or reformat, see the gloss skill\". The skill does not describe the new rule and still says `add` warns only for groups rated below their floor (`SKILL.md:106`). `h1` only touches the README, so the skill was not brought in line. Affects `warn`, `readme`. Verdict on the skill text: the skill needs the same update, or the warning's pointer should go.\n\n2. **Mismatch, read.** The message says exempt groups are \"a pure rename or reformat\", but `src/floor.ts` exempts every `mechanical` group whose floor is `module` or below, not only renames or reformats. Import rewrites and similar follow-on edits pass silently. Either the wording or the condition should change. Affects `warn`. This is low severity, and the current behaviour matches the spec's list of mechanical edits.\n\n3. **Missing risk, read.** The warning fires on every `add` for any existing gloss with unrated non-mechanical groups, including `--dry-run`. Scripts or CI that treat stderr as failure will see new output. The change's own annotation has no `impact` on `warn`, `test`, or `readme`, so running `add` on this change would warn about all three. That is consistent with the rule, but the author should either rate them or expect the warning. Affects `warn`.\n\n4. **Test gaps, read.** Three things are untested:\n   - The `and N more` truncation branch (more than 10 unrated groups).\n   - The `floored` list when several groups carry a floor signal.\n   - The `all groups rated` test checks only that no warning appears; it does not check the exit code.\n\n   The \"what is stored\" test only checks that the notes contain no `\"impact\"` string. It passes trivially when no impact is written. Affects `test`.\n\n5. **Simplification, read.** The `impactRank(f.floor) > impactRank('module')` floor-signal test now appears in two loops in `ratingProblems`. A small helper, such as `floorSignal(id)`, would remove the duplication. Affects `warn`.\n\n6. **Necessity grading.** The labels look accurate. `warn` is required, because without it `add` never names unrated groups. `test` and `readme` are supporting, serving `warn`. Nothing here looks incidental, defensive or speculative.\n\n7. **Removal candidates.** None. The mechanical exemption is what the task asks for, and the `floored` detail is part of the stated design.\n\n**Verified:**\n- The five impact tests pass (`node --test test/impactwarn.test.ts`).\n- The signals tests pass, including the existing `ratingProblems` assertions, which the new warning does not disturb (exit 0).\n- The warning does not change the exit code or the stored notes (covered by the impact test that passes).\n- `ratingProblems` is called only from `add`, so `show`, `html` and other commands do not emit the warning. Confirmed by grep.\n\n**Read only:** findings 1–5 come from reading the code and the skill. I did not run the truncation branch or the skill-versus-warning contradiction in a scratch repo.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES\n\nThe background run finished. I'm updating my earlier report with the results I didn't have then.\n\n**Full suite and typecheck, now complete:**\n- `tsc` exits 0.\n- The full `npm test` run has one failure, `performance: signals over 800+ hunks finishes within a few seconds`, which took 5.25 s. **Verified:** rerun alone, the same test passes